 "text": "**Nationwide FlexDirect currently offers the highest headline rate on a UK current account balance: 5% AER (fixed) on up to £1,500 for the first 12 months.**\n\nThis is confirmed on Nationwide’s site and in recent comparison tables (as of mid-to-late November 2026). After 12 months it falls to 1% AER (variable) on the same capped balance. Key conditions include paying in at least £1,500 a month (transfers from other Nationwide accounts and refunds do not count) and it generally being your first FlexDirect in the last three years. There is no monthly fee. New customers may also qualify for cashback (up to £120 in the first year under spending/Direct Debit rules) and a switching incentive.\n\n### Strong alternatives, especially for larger balances\nThe “best” account depends on how much you keep in the account, because the 5% rate is tightly capped:\n\n- **LHV Bank Current Account** — 4.00% AER (variable; tracks the Bank of England base rate) on balances up to £1,000,000. No monthly fee and fewer usage restrictions. App-based. Official LHV pages confirm the current 4% rate.\n- **Kroo Current Account** — 3.25% AER (variable) for new customers on balances up to £500,000. This includes a fixed 2% bonus for the first 12 months; it then reverts to a lower tracker rate. No monthly fee and fee-free spending abroad in many cases.\n\nOther accounts (such as some Zopa, Virgin Money, Santander Edge Up, or Club Lloyds options) pay lower rates, often with balance caps, fees, or stricter conditions. Most high-street current accounts still pay 0% on credit balances.\n\n### Important caveats\nDedicated easy-access savings accounts frequently pay comparable or higher rates (often in the 4.5–5%+ range) without requiring the account to function as your everyday current account. For money you do not need for day-to-day spending, a separate savings account is usually more efficient.\n\nRates, bonuses, and conditions change frequently. Always check the provider’s website for the latest terms, eligibility (UK residency, age 18+, etc.), and full details before applying. Deposits are generally protected by the FSCS up to £120,000 per person per banking licence. Interest may be taxable above your Personal Savings Allowance.\n\nThis is general information based on publicly available rates, not personal financial advice.",
